WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:06.080
The last type of error we will cover occurs
when one tries to run code; hence, this type

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.550
of error is called a runtime error.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.980
Runtime errors are not syntax errors, nor
are they logic errors.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:17.130
They are the type of errors that occur when
one tries to do with an app what cannot be

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.820
done as the app’s code is being run.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.430
Runtime errors can also stem from logic errors.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:25.980
It’s essential to distinguish among them.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.570
Syntax errors trigger a “SyntaxError”
message.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:34.390
Logic errors don't produce any immediate error
messages but may lead to runtime errors.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.950
Runtime errors generate various Python error
codes.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:41.890
Additionally, runtime errors can sometimes
be mitigated through proper error-handling

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.360
techniques that we will cover in the next
set of topics.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.610
One such example of a runtime error is in
this 513-runtime file.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:57.290
There are no syntax errors that I can see,
and logically, all we are trying to do is

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:04.800
retrieve, on lines 2 and 3, the first and
third items on this list of plant types that

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:08.330
could be covered in a biology class.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.720
Can you spot the potential runtime error here?

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.970
Let’s run this code.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:22.939
We see a fairly common runtime error telling
us that for plant type 3, the list index is

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:25.820
out of range.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:27.250
What does that mean?

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.659
Let’s go back and look at the code.

00:01:29.659 --> 00:01:37.869
We have three plant types on line 1, but remember
that when referring to list items, the list

00:01:37.869 --> 00:01:44.340
is zero-based, meaning that the first item
is item zero, the second item is item 1, and

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.010
so forth.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:51.570
Thus, getting the item index of three is trying
to get an item that is out of the range of

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:56.210
this list, hence the list index out of range
error.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:02.439
Another point to make about the error is that
we see a Traceback indicator, which is an

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:04.979
error report showing where the problem occurred.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:11.660
In this case, the problem is on line 3 in
the code.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.460
To fix this problem and get the correct two
plant items to display, we need to change

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:21.860
the index numbers on lines 2 and 3 to 0 and
2, respectively.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:34.700
I will make the change and rerun the code,
and the code now works with no runtime errors.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.580
The takeaway here is that the emphasis on
learning runtime errors is understanding what

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.330
each runtime error means and knowing how to
read a traceback to see what line number has

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.880
code that is causing the runtime error to
occur.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.250
Understanding what a runtime error means can
lead to less time troubleshooting, which leaves

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:56.530
more time to do more fun things, like developing
more apps.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:01.290
Now that we have covered the three types of
errors, let’s use this knowledge to analyze

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.780
and then fix a code example.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:12.909
This file, 513-analyze, replicates a code
example we worked on elsewhere in the course.

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:19.030
Use the Review 5.1 workbook project to answer
questions and perform possible fixes related

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.580
to this file.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.750
I will have the answers for you in the next
video.

